  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n0 0 0\n1 0 0\n0 0 0\nOutput:\n2 0 0\n0 0 0\n0 0 0\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n0 1 0\n1 0 0\n0 0 0\nOutput:\n2 2 0\n0 0 0\n0 0 0\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n0 0 1\n0 0 0\n1 0 0\nOutput:\n2 2 0\n0 0 0\n0 0 0\n\nExample 4:\n\nInput:\n0 1 0\n0 0 1\n0 0 0\nOutput:\n2 2 0\n0 0 0\n0 0 0\n\nExample 5:\n\nInput:\n0 0 1\n0 0 0\n0 0 0\nOutput:\n2 0 0\n0 0 0\n0 0 0\n\nExample 6:\n\nInput:\n1 1 0\n0 0 0\n1 0 0\nOutput:\n2 2 2\n0 0 0\n0 0 0\n\nExample 7:\n\nInput:\n0 1 0\n1 1 0\n0 0 0\nOutput:\n2 2 2\n0 0 0\n0 0 0\n\nExample 8:\n\nInput:\n1 1 0\n0 0 0\n1 0 1\nOutput:\n2 2 2\n0 2 0\n0 0 0\n\nExample 9:\n\nInput:\n0 1 0\n1 1 0\n1 0 0\nOutput:\n2 2 2\n0 2 0\n0 0 0\n\nExample 10:\n\nInput:\n1 0 0\n0 0 1\n0 1 1\nOutput:\n2 2 2\n0 2 0\n0 0 0\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n0 1 0\n0 0 0\n0 1 0\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    Transform the input grid by moving all non\u2011zero cells (treated as \u201c1\u201d)\n    to the top of the grid, left\u2011aligning the first row and centering any\n    remaining cells in the rows below. The moved cells are written as \u201c2\u201d.\n    \n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    grid : list[list[int]]\n        Input grid containing only 0 and 1 (or other non\u2011zero values).\n    \n    Returns\n    -------\n    list[list[int]]\n        Transformed grid with the same dimensions.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not grid:\n        return []\n\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n\n    # Count non\u2011zero cells (the \u201cpieces\u201d to be moved)\n    total = sum(1 for row in grid for v in row if v != 0)\n\n    # Prepare output filled with zeros\n    out = [[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n\n    remaining = total\n    r = 0                     # current row we are filling\n\n    while remaining > 0 and r < rows:\n        # Number of cells we can place in this row\n        items = min(cols, remaining)\n\n        if r == 0:\n            # First row is always left\u2011aligned\n            start = 0\n        else:\n            # Subsequent rows are centered\n            start = (cols - items) // 2\n\n        # Write the value 2 in the chosen positions\n        for j in range(items):\n            out[r][start + j] = 2\n\n        remaining -= items\n        r += 1\n\n    return out",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    Transform the input grid by moving all non\u2011zero cells (treated as \u201c1\u201d)\n    to the top of the grid, left\u2011aligning the first row and centering any\n    remaining cells in the rows below. The moved cells are written as \u201c2\u201d.\n    \n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    grid : list[list[int]]\n        Input grid containing only 0 and 1 (or other non\u2011zero values).\n    \n    Returns\n    -------\n    list[list[int]]\n        Transformed grid with the same dimensions.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not grid:\n        return []\n\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n\n    # Count non\u2011zero cells (the \u201cpieces\u201d to be moved)\n    total = sum(1 for row in grid for v in row if v != 0)\n\n    # Prepare output filled with zeros\n    out = [[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n\n    remaining = total\n    r = 0                     # current row we are filling\n\n    while remaining > 0 and r < rows:\n        # Number of cells we can place in this row\n        items = min(cols, remaining)\n\n        if r == 0:\n            # First row is always left\u2011aligned\n            start = 0\n        else:\n            # Subsequent rows are centered\n            start = (cols - items) // 2\n\n        # Write the value 2 in the chosen positions\n        for j in range(items):\n            out[r][start + j] = 2\n\n        remaining -= items\n        r += 1\n\n    return out",
